# Flowcharts: a recipe, to copy and adapt (pikslide --help flowchart).
#
# A flowchart follows a program step by step: what it does, where it
# decides and where it loops. This one draws quicksort, sorting A[lo..hi]
# in place: it partitions the range round a pivot, in a loop with a
# decision in it, then sorts each side by calling itself.
#
# THE SYMBOLS, AND WHAT DRAWS EACH
# start, end: rounded ends -- oval "Return" fit
# process: a rectangle -- box "j = j + 1" fit
# decision: a diamond, a "yes" and a "no" leaving it -- diamond "j < hi" wid 2in ht 0.75in
# predefined process, such as a call: a rectangle with doubled sides --
#   shape flowChartPredefinedProcess "quicksort(A, lo, i - 1)" wid 2.2in ht 0.5in
# input, output: a parallelogram -- shape flowChartInputOutput "print i" wid 1.6in ht 0.45in
# flow: an arrow -- arrow, arrow "yes" ljust
#
# HOW IT'S LAID OUT
# - The main path runs straight down: after `down`, each arrow and shape
#   goes below the one before, with no coordinates. The branches and the
#   loop are drawn afterwards, from the shapes' edges.
# - Decisions are one size: the first has its wid and ht, and the others
#   copy them with `same`. PowerPoint sets a diamond's text in the middle
#   half of its width, so a diamond is a bit over twice as wide as its
#   widest line; pikslide warns when it isn't. Calls are one size too.
# - Label each exit of a decision: `ljust` beside one going down, `above`
#   one going right. An exit that bends is a short labeled `line`, then an
#   `arrow` on from where it ends: a bent line's label would sit on none of
#   its segments (pikslide --help line).
# - The loop goes back round the left, and the way out of the whole round
#   the right, through points made from the shapes' edges and kept in
#   variables (leftx, rightx), so that the lines move with the shapes.
# - What follows the loop is a second column, far enough right to clear
#   the "no" that bypasses the swap.
# - A line to a parallelogram's side ends a tenth of its width in from its
#   .w or .e: its slanted sides start a fifth of its width in, and .w and
#   .e are its box's. inset = Out.wid / 10, then arrow to Out.w + (inset, 0).
# - Swimlanes: a tall box per actor, behind the first shape, and each step
#   placed in its lane with at (Lane.x, ...).

down
Start: oval "quicksort(A, lo, hi)" fit
arrow
Check: diamond "lo < hi" wid 2in ht 0.75in
arrow "yes" ljust
Init: box "pivot = A[hi]" "i = lo" "j = lo" fit
arrow
Loop: diamond "j < hi" same
arrow "yes" ljust
Less: diamond "A[j] < pivot" same
arrow "yes" ljust
Swap: box "swap A[i], A[j]" "i = i + 1" fit
arrow
Next: box "j = j + 1" fit

# Back round the left, to the loop's test.
leftx = Loop.w.x - 0.3in
arrow from Next.s down 0.25in then to (leftx, Next.s.y - 0.25in) then to (leftx, Loop.y) then to Loop.w

# Not less: past the swap, down the right, into j = j + 1.
line "no" above from Less.e right 0.4in
arrow down until even with Next then to Next.e

# Out of the loop: the pivot into place, then each side sorted.
Place: box "swap A[i], A[hi]" fit with .w at 1.2in right of Loop.e
arrow "no" above from Loop.e to Place.w
arrow down from Place.s
Lower: shape flowChartPredefinedProcess "quicksort(A, lo, i - 1)" wid 2.2in ht 0.5in
arrow
Upper: shape flowChartPredefinedProcess "quicksort(A, i + 1, hi)" same
arrow
Return: oval "Return" fit

# Nothing to sort: round the right, to the end.
rightx = Lower.e.x + 0.3in
line "no" above from Check.e right 0.4in
arrow to (rightx, Check.y) then to (rightx, Return.y) then to Return.e
